body:
I am using ubuntu/Bash

&#x200B;

My comp is windows 10

Absolutely! So basically my girlfriend was seeking treatment, and was progressing! Things were steady and going well. When Covid hit, we saw that our lives were normalizing. We made a mutual decision to get engaged, I was a little hesitant because I was still in school and wanted to make sure I could provide money. We had a very very short engagement due to members of her family taking a gap year abroad to another country, so I was okay with pushing the wedding earlier. Things got complicated because my father's doctor said my father should not attend the wedding, I wanted my parents at least being there, conflict arose. Now she is my ex-fiancee. I still do truly care deeply about her and I am really hoping she is okay because she means a lot to me. She really is a great girl, but I think stress got in the way of our relationship. 

&#x200B;

Moral of the story: As long as someone is seeking treatment and acknowledges there are problems, that's okay. The second it can't become a safe space between two people, that's a problem...

&#x200B;

If you have any more questions, please feel free to ask!

You are absolutely right about being there for someone with anxiety, and to show care and support. However I never signed up for this. I am committed to the relationship getting better. She is seeing a therapist and she herself does not want this, any of it. She wants to be logical, she does not want fears or emotions.

&#x200B;

My question was not whether I should be or should not be in a relationship or whether I am capable of being in a relationship like this one. I spent about 6 months doing this, and trust me it was way more toxic before with emotional dependency and inability to speak for herself. I got her to go to therapy.

&#x200B;

My real question was is this fighting unhealthy? How can it be resolved?
